programs.haiku:
"""layers.0.mlp.down_proj:945 -- a weak, mostly token-identity feature.

Its activation is small (std 0.034) and has no clean concept behind it: the sign and size
depend on which token is being read, in a diffuse way (about 60% of the variance is between
tokens, spread over thousands of them). This program captures only the coarse trends that
hold on training text, as a linear score of a few surface features of the current token:

- a token containing a partial UTF-8 byte (shown as U+FFFD) tends to push the component up;
- a whitespace-only token, a digit, or a CJK character tends to push it slightly down;
- a word-initial token (leading space) pushes it slightly up;
- code punctuation (. _ / ( ) etc.) pushes it slightly down.

Coefficients are least-squares fits on 1024 training rows. The program's variance explained is
close to zero, so it is an honest weak description, not a faithful model of the component.
"""

import re

READS = ()

CJK = re.compile("[぀-ヿ一-鿿]")
CODE_PUNCT = re.compile(r"[._/()\[\]{}<>=;:]")
DIGIT = re.compile(r"\d")


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    s = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    value = 0.0
    if "�" in s:
        value += 0.0154
    if CJK.search(s):
        value -= 0.0011
    if s.startswith(" "):
        value += 0.0020
    if CODE_PUNCT.search(s):
        value -= 0.0012
    if s.strip() == "":
        value -= 0.0021
    if DIGIT.search(s):
        value -= 0.0020
    return carry, float(value)

